INT. PARIS CATACOMBS - OSSUARY CHAMBER, 1793

Walls of stacked skulls. A single torch. Water drips from the vaulted ceiling.

CITIZEN AUGUSTIN (40s, revolutionary sash, unpowdered hair, stiff with fear) stands alone at a stone table, copying names into a ledger. He mutters as he writes.

AUGUSTIN
Lefèvre. Marchand. Girard.

A footstep echoes. He freezes. Slowly closes the ledger.

AUGUSTIN (CONT'D)
Who goes there?

CELESTINE (30s, cloaked, hood up, a lantern shielded under her arm) steps out from behind a pillar of bones.

CELESTINE
A friend of the dead.

AUGUSTIN
There are no friends in the Republic.

CELESTINE
Then I am no friend. Only a sister.

AUGUSTIN
Whose sister?

CELESTINE
The one you have on that list.

He glances at the ledger. She comes closer. The light catches her face.

AUGUSTIN
You should not be down here.

CELESTINE
Neither should you.

AUGUSTIN
I am an officer of the Committee.

CELESTINE
You are a clerk with a candle.

AUGUSTIN
Madame...

CELESTINE
Citizen. Celestine Duval.

AUGUSTIN
Citizen Duval, I could have you arrested.

CELESTINE
Then do it. Call the guards. They are above, drinking wine from your cellar.

He says nothing. Wipes his palms on his coat.

CELESTINE (CONT'D)
My brother's name is on that page. Pierre Duval.

AUGUSTIN
I do not control the lists.

CELESTINE
You hold the pen.

AUGUSTIN
The pen obeys.

CELESTINE
So does the guillotine. Until someone stops it.

Water drips. He opens the ledger. Finds the page. Looks at her. Looks at the page.

AUGUSTIN
Pierre Duval. Printer.

CELESTINE
Yes.

AUGUSTIN
Condemned for a pamphlet.

CELESTINE
For an opinion.

He tears the page out. Folds it. Hands it to her.

AUGUSTIN
There is a carriage at the Barrière d'Enfer at midnight.

CELESTINE
Why?

AUGUSTIN
Because I am tired of counting skulls.
